    "doc": "conditions",
    "section": "p2",
    "page": 2,
    "heading": "Working hours",
    "text": "Your normal working hours will be an average of 40 per week, this includes a 30-minute unpaid break for each working day where you work six hours or more. The normal hours are worked, over five days, Monday to Sunday inclusive on a shift basis, between the hours of 6.30am and 2.00am the following day. However, you are expected to work the hours necessary to fulfil the objectives of your role.",
    "topics": [
      "working hours",
      "shift status",
      "forty hours"
    ],
    "status": "DOCUMENTED FACT",
    "verified": "2026-10-08",
    "title": "Working hours",
    "sectionId": "p2",
    "filename": "Conditions of Employment .pdf",
    "context": "[PDF page 2; public edition with explicit redactions]\n\nWORKING HOURS\nYour normal working hours will be an average of 40 per week, this includes a 30-minute\nunpaid break for each working day where you work six hours or more. The normal hours are\nworked, over five days, Monday to Sunday inclusive on a shift basis, between the hours of\n6.30am and 2.00am the following day. However, you are expected to work the hours\nnecessary to fulfil the objectives of your role. You may be requested to work reasonable\nadditional hours according to the needs of the business. Your agreement to these hours\nshould not be unreasonably withheld. Your position entitles you to receive payment at your\nnormal hourly rate of pay for additional hours, so long as these have been authorised in\nadvance by the General Manager or Head Chef.\nYour working hours may be varied where such changes are necessary according to the\nneeds of the business. Reasonable notice of any changes will be given where possible.\nThe company will record your actual hours worked in accordance with the Working Time\nRegulations. We ask you to also record your actual hours worked and if you find yourself\nworking on average, more than 48 hours per week over a 17 week period or have any other\nconcerns about your rights under Working Time Regulations, to speak to your line manager.\nPlease refer to your employee handbook for your rights under the Working Time regulations.\nSHORT TIME WORKING & LAYOFFS\nThe Company reserves the right to:\n1. Make temporary reductions in your weekly working hours if this is considered\nnecessary to manage anticipated quiet trading periods. You will be given at least one\nweek’s notice of any reductions.\n2. Lay you off without pay if it is considered necessary. You will be given not less than\ntwo weeks’ notice of any such layoffs. For the first five days of any layoff, you will\nreceive the statutory minimum payment applicable. In the event that you are laid off or\nput on short time working, your entitlement to pay on workless days in that period of\nlay-off or short time working will cease and instead, if you qualify, you will be paid\nguarantee payments at the prevailing statutory rate during that period in accordance\nwith statutory requirements.\nHOLIDAY ENTITLEMENT\nThe holiday year runs from 1st March to 28 February (29 in a leap year). If your\nemployment starts or finishes part way through the holiday year, your holiday entitlement\nduring that year shall be calculated on a pro-rata basis. In each annual holiday year, you are\nentitled to take 28 days paid leave, including the eight statutory holidays (or the pro-rata\nequivalent if you work part-time), where one day equals one fifth of your normal working\nweek. Holiday accrues at 1/52 of your annual entitlement for each completed week of\nservice. Details of entitlements are found in your Employee Handbook. All holiday pay is\npaid at normal basic rate. You will receive no additional payment or time in lieu if you are\nrostered to work on a Bank Holiday. Any holiday not taken each holiday year will not be\ntaken into the next year.\n2",
    "note": "PDF page numbers refer to file page order. Personal details are redacted in this public edition; consult the original file on your device for its layout. A source provision does not establish what occurred in the incident."
